# Choose a cropper

Choose the interaction first: should the user move the selection, move the photo under a fixed frame, or resize a frame that settles back into place?

## Movable selection

`Cropper` supplies a movable, resizable rectangle when you omit children. Use a child stencil to configure its shape and controls.

<Example source={CropperTypesExampleSource} title="CropperTypesExample">
	<CropperTypesExample src={image('corgi-lying-down.jpg')} />
</Example>

## Fixed selection

Use `FixedCropper` with a `stencilSize`. Its size is measured in layout pixels; zoom changes how many image pixels are inside that frame. To make the frame inert, explicitly hide and disable the child stencil's controls.

<Example source={FixedCropperTypeExampleSource} title="FixedCropperTypeExample">
	<FixedCropperTypeExample
		size="medium"
		src={image('dog-on-grass.jpg')}
		stencilSize={{ width: 400, height: 400 }}
		imageRestriction={ImageRestriction.stencil}
	>
		<RectangleStencil handlers={false} lines={false} movable={false} resizable={false} />
	</FixedCropperTypeExample>
</Example>

`ImageRestriction.stencil` keeps the photo covering the selection. A stencil-size function can adapt the frame to the boundary, and oversized requests are fitted within it. See [defaults and restrictions](/docs/guides/defaults-and-restrictions#fixed-stencil-size).

## A frame that settles after interaction

Leave the fixed cropper's stencil interactive when users should be able to resize it before it settles back to its configured size.

<Example source={FixedCropperTypeExampleSource} title="FixedCropperTypeExample">
	<FixedCropperTypeExample
		src={image('three-puppies.jpg')}
		stencilSize={{ width: 280, height: 280 }}
		imageRestriction={ImageRestriction.stencil}
	/>
</Example>

For a more specialized mobile interface, compose a toolbar through a wrapper snippet and supply core algorithms. The [showcase](/docs/showcase) includes a Telegram-style editor and a Twitter-style avatar cropper. The [absolute zoom tutorial](/docs/tutorials/absolute-zoom) explains the fixed-frame slider in detail.
